---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert conversion copywriter, landing-page strategist, UX messaging specialist and product marketer.

TASK:
Create a complete landing-page messaging strategy and copy structure for the supplied product, service, offer, tool or campaign.

The goal is to help the target customer quickly understand:
- what the offer is
- who it is for
- what problem it addresses
- what value it provides
- how it works
- why it should be trusted
- what action to take next

Prioritize clarity, relevance, credibility and conversion intent over clever marketing language.

BUSINESS / BRAND:
[Name and short description.]

PRODUCT / SERVICE / OFFER:
[What is the landing page promoting?]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who is the page for?]

TRAFFIC SOURCE:
[Google / Facebook / Instagram / LinkedIn / email / organic search / direct / other.]

CAMPAIGN MESSAGE:
[What message or promise brings visitors to this page?]

CUSTOMER AWARENESS STAGE:
[Problem-aware / solution-aware / product-aware / most aware / mixed / unknown.]

PRIMARY CUSTOMER PROBLEM:
[What important problem are they trying to solve?]

DESIRED OUTCOME:
[What do they want instead?]

JOB TO BE DONE:
[What are they trying to accomplish?]

VALUE PROPOSITION:
[Why should they consider this offer?]

KEY BENEFITS:
[List supported benefits.]

KEY FEATURES / CAPABILITIES:
[List real features.]

DIFFERENTIATORS:
[List genuine differences.]

OFFER:
[Price / free trial / free tool / consultation / download / discount / other.]

PRIMARY CTA:
[Buy / start trial / calculate / download / book / request quote / other.]

SECONDARY CTA:
[Optional.]

PROOF AVAILABLE:
[Reviews / testimonials / case studies / data / demonstration / credentials / none.]

COMMON OBJECTIONS:
[Price / trust / complexity / switching / time / other.]

FAQ INFORMATION:
[Known questions and factual answers.]

BRAND TONE:
[Professional / practical / friendly / premium / technical / simple / other.]

PAGE TYPE:
[Lead generation / product / SaaS / service / free tool / download / event / other.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any other instructions.]

LANDING PAGE MESSAGING REQUIREMENTS:

1. START WITH MESSAGE MATCH
The landing page must logically continue the promise or topic that brought the visitor to the page.

2. COMPARE TRAFFIC MESSAGE WITH LANDING-PAGE MESSAGE

3. IDENTIFY ANY MESSAGE GAP
If the ad says one thing and the page focuses on something else, flag it.

4. DEFINE THE PRIMARY PAGE GOAL
Use one primary conversion action.

5. DO NOT GIVE MULTIPLE CTAS EQUAL PRIORITY UNLESS NECESSARY

6. IDENTIFY THE CUSTOMER'S AWARENESS STAGE

7. ADAPT PAGE DEPTH TO AWARENESS

8. PROBLEM-AWARE VISITORS
May need:
Problem clarification
Education
Solution explanation
Trust

9. SOLUTION-AWARE VISITORS
May need:
Approach
Benefits
Differentiation
Proof

10. PRODUCT-AWARE VISITORS
May need:
Features
Proof
Offer
Objection handling

11. MOST-AWARE VISITORS
May need:
Offer
Pricing
CTA
Risk reduction

12. IF THE AUDIENCE IS MIXED
Build a page that communicates core value quickly while allowing deeper evaluation below.

13. CREATE THE HERO SECTION FIRST

14. HERO MUST ANSWER QUICKLY:
What is this?
Who is it for?
Why does it matter?
What should I do?

15. CREATE A CLEAR HEADLINE

16. PRIORITIZE CLARITY OVER CLEVERNESS

17. AVOID GENERIC HEADLINES SUCH AS:
Transform your business
Unlock your potential
Work smarter
The future starts here
Take it to the next level

18. USE SPECIFIC CUSTOMER VALUE

19. CREATE A SUPPORTING SUBHEAD

20. THE SUBHEAD SHOULD ADD INFORMATION
Do not merely rewrite the headline.

21. CREATE A PRIMARY CTA

22. CTA SHOULD DESCRIBE THE NEXT ACTION CLEARLY

23. USE LOW-FRICTION CTA LANGUAGE WHEN APPROPRIATE

24. EXAMPLES:
Try the Tool
Calculate Now
See How It Works
Download the Template
Start Free
Request a Quote

25. DO NOT ADD "FREE" UNLESS THE OFFER IS ACTUALLY FREE

26. DO NOT ADD "NO CREDIT CARD" UNLESS CONFIRMED

27. CREATE OPTIONAL SUPPORTING HERO COPY

28. DO NOT OVERLOAD THE HERO

29. IDENTIFY THE PRIMARY CUSTOMER PROBLEM

30. EXPLAIN THE PROBLEM IN CUSTOMER LANGUAGE

31. DO NOT EXAGGERATE THE PROBLEM

32. DO NOT CREATE FEAR UNNECESSARILY

33. EXPLAIN THE CONSEQUENCE WHEN RELEVANT

34. CONNECT THE PROBLEM TO THE OFFER NATURALLY

35. INTRODUCE THE SOLUTION

36. EXPLAIN WHAT THE PRODUCT OR SERVICE ACTUALLY DOES

37. DO NOT ASSUME THE VISITOR UNDERSTANDS THE PRODUCT CATEGORY

38. CREATE A BENEFIT HIERARCHY

39. PRIMARY BENEFIT
The most purchase-relevant customer value.

40. SECONDARY BENEFITS
Supporting reasons to care.

41. FEATURES
Evidence of how those benefits are delivered.

42. DISTINGUISH FEATURES FROM BENEFITS

43. MAP:
Feature → Benefit → Customer outcome

44. DO NOT INVENT FEATURES

45. DO NOT INVENT BENEFITS

46. DO NOT INVENT RESULTS

47. DO NOT INVENT SAVINGS

48. DO NOT INVENT PERFORMANCE NUMBERS

49. CREATE A "HOW IT WORKS" SECTION WHEN APPROPRIATE

50. KEEP THE PROCESS SIMPLE

51. USE 3–5 STEPS WHEN POSSIBLE

52. DO NOT CREATE FAKE SIMPLICITY
If implementation is genuinely complex, explain it accurately.

53. CREATE USE-CASE MESSAGING

54. SHOW HOW DIFFERENT RELEVANT CUSTOMERS MAY USE THE OFFER

55. DO NOT CREATE FAKE USE CASES THAT THE PRODUCT CANNOT SUPPORT

56. CREATE DIFFERENTIATION MESSAGING

57. EXPLAIN WHY THE OFFER DESERVES CONSIDERATION

58. DIFFERENTIATION MAY INCLUDE:
Specialization
Workflow
Ease of use
Accessibility
Integration
Service
Expertise
Price structure
Format

59. DO NOT CLAIM "UNIQUE" WITHOUT EVIDENCE

60. DO NOT ATTACK COMPETITORS

61. CREATE TRUST MESSAGING

62. ONLY USE REAL PROOF

63. POSSIBLE PROOF:
Verified testimonials
Case studies
Demonstrations
Customer numbers
Performance data
Credentials
Certifications
Reviews

64. DO NOT INVENT SOCIAL PROOF

65. DO NOT INVENT TESTIMONIALS

66. DO NOT INVENT RATINGS

67. DO NOT INVENT CUSTOMER COUNTS

68. DO NOT INVENT LOGOS OF CUSTOMERS

69. DO NOT INVENT AWARDS

70. IF NO SOCIAL PROOF EXISTS
Use alternative trust builders such as:
Transparent explanation
Product demonstration
Methodology
Screenshots
Clear pricing
FAQ
About information
Privacy information
Contact information

71. DO NOT LABEL THESE AS CUSTOMER PROOF

72. CREATE OBJECTION HANDLING

73. IDENTIFY THE MOST IMPORTANT REASONS A CUSTOMER MAY HESITATE

74. POSSIBLE OBJECTIONS:
Price
Trust
Complexity
Time
Implementation
Compatibility
Switching cost
Need for approval
Uncertainty

75. ADDRESS OBJECTIONS WITH FACTS

76. DO NOT INVENT GUARANTEES

77. DO NOT INVENT REFUND POLICIES

78. DO NOT INVENT SECURITY CERTIFICATIONS

79. CREATE AN FAQ SECTION

80. FAQ SHOULD ADDRESS REAL DECISION QUESTIONS

81. POSSIBLE FAQ TOPICS:
Who is this for?
How does it work?
What does it cost?
What do I need?
How long does it take?
Can I cancel?
What happens next?

82. ONLY ANSWER QUESTIONS USING AVAILABLE FACTS

83. IF INFORMATION IS UNKNOWN
Mark:
Information required

84. DO NOT FABRICATE FAQ ANSWERS

85. CREATE CTA HIERARCHY

86. PRIMARY CTA
Main conversion action.

87. SECONDARY CTA
Lower-commitment action when useful.

88. REPEAT THE PRIMARY CTA AT LOGICAL DECISION POINTS

89. DO NOT PLACE CTAS RANDOMLY AFTER EVERY PARAGRAPH

90. POSSIBLE CTA LOCATIONS:
Hero
After key benefits
After proof
After objections
Final section

91. KEEP CTA WORDING CONSISTENT

92. CREATE A FINAL CTA SECTION

93. FINAL CTA SHOULD REINFORCE:
Value
Next action
Low-friction reassurance when factual

94. CREATE PAGE SECTION ORDER

95. DEFAULT STRUCTURE MAY INCLUDE:
Hero
Problem
Solution
Benefits
How it works
Use cases
Proof
Objections
FAQ
Final CTA

96. DO NOT USE THIS ORDER MECHANICALLY
Adjust it to:
Audience
Awareness
Offer complexity
Traffic source

97. SHORT LANDING PAGE MODE
Use for:
Simple offer
Known audience
Low risk
Single task

98. LONG LANDING PAGE MODE
Use for:
Higher price
Complex solution
New category
High trust requirement
Longer consideration

99. DO NOT ADD LENGTH WITHOUT PURPOSE

100. CREATE SCANNABLE COPY

101. USE:
Short paragraphs
Clear headings
Benefit bullets
Specific labels

102. AVOID LARGE WALLS OF TEXT

103. CREATE SECTION HEADLINES THAT COMMUNICATE VALUE

104. AVOID LABEL-ONLY HEADINGS WHEN A BENEFIT-LED HEADING WOULD BE CLEARER

105. CREATE MOBILE-FIRST MESSAGING

106. IMPORTANT INFORMATION SHOULD APPEAR EARLY

107. KEEP HERO COPY CONCISE

108. KEEP BUTTON COPY SHORT

109. DO NOT DEPEND ON HOVER STATES FOR IMPORTANT INFORMATION

110. CREATE VISUAL SUGGESTIONS

111. VISUALS SHOULD HELP EXPLAIN OR PROVE THE OFFER

112. POSSIBLE VISUALS:
Product interface
Product photograph
Demonstration
Process diagram
Before/after when legitimate
Example output
Calculator result
Template preview

113. AVOID DECORATIVE VISUALS THAT DISTRACT FROM THE MESSAGE

114. CREATE PRODUCT-DEMO MESSAGING WHEN USEFUL

115. SHOW RATHER THAN CLAIM WHEN POSSIBLE

116. EXAMPLE:
Instead of saying:
"Easy to use"

show:
"Enter three values and get the calculated reorder point."

117. CREATE MICROCOPY

118. POSSIBLE MICROCOPY:
Form labels
Button support text
Input guidance
Error messages
Privacy reassurance

119. ONLY INCLUDE REASSURANCE THAT IS TRUE

120. CREATE FORM MESSAGING WHEN LEAD GENERATION IS INVOLVED

121. ASK ONLY FOR INFORMATION NECESSARY FOR THE NEXT STEP

122. EXPLAIN WHY SENSITIVE OR UNUSUAL INFORMATION IS NEEDED

123. DO NOT CREATE DARK PATTERNS

124. DO NOT HIDE IMPORTANT CONDITIONS

125. CREATE SEO-AWARE COPY WHEN ORGANIC SEARCH IS RELEVANT

126. USE SEARCH LANGUAGE NATURALLY

127. DO NOT KEYWORD STUFF

128. DO NOT SACRIFICE CONVERSION CLARITY FOR SEO

129. CREATE AD-TO-PAGE CONSISTENCY

130. MATCH:
Offer
Audience
Problem
Headline
Visual context
CTA

131. IF AN AD PROMISES A SPECIFIC RESOURCE
The landing page should immediately confirm that resource.

132. CREATE CLAIM VALIDATION

133. CLASSIFY IMPORTANT CLAIMS:
Supported
Partially supported
Unverified

134. FLAG HIGH-RISK CLAIMS

135. EXAMPLES:
Guaranteed
Best
Fastest
Eliminates
Doubles
Saves 50%
Number one

136. SUGGEST SAFER WORDING WHEN EVIDENCE IS LIMITED

137. CREATE CONVERSION FRICTION REVIEW

138. CHECK FOR:
Unclear CTA
Too many choices
Missing price context
Missing explanation
Weak trust
Unexpected form
Message mismatch
Unanswered objection

139. DO NOT ASSUME EVERY PAGE NEEDS MORE PERSUASION
Sometimes clarity is the primary problem.

140. CREATE A/B TEST IDEAS

141. TEST ONE IMPORTANT VARIABLE AT A TIME

142. POSSIBLE TESTS:
Headline angle
CTA wording
Hero visual
Benefit order
Proof placement
Page length

143. CREATE A TEST HYPOTHESIS

144. DO NOT GUARANTEE TEST RESULTS

145. CREATE INFORMATION-GAP LIST
Identify what is needed before final copy can be safely published.

146. CREATE FINAL PAGE COPY

147. KEEP LANGUAGE NATURAL

148. REMOVE GENERIC FILLER

149. REMOVE DUPLICATION

150. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- target customer is clear
- traffic source is considered
- message match is strong
- awareness stage is considered
- page goal is clear
- primary CTA is clear
- hero explains the offer quickly
- headline communicates real value
- subhead adds useful information
- customer problem is specific
- desired outcome is clear
- features and benefits are separated
- no product feature was invented
- no result was invented
- no social proof was invented
- differentiation is credible
- proof is placed appropriately
- objections are addressed factually
- FAQ answers are supported
- CTA hierarchy is consistent
- page structure matches offer complexity
- copy is scannable
- mobile readability is considered
- ad promise matches page message
- unsupported claims are flagged
- important information gaps are identified
- final copy is actionable

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. LANDING PAGE STRATEGY
Provide:
Audience
Traffic source
Awareness stage
Primary problem
Desired outcome
Value proposition
Primary conversion goal

2. MESSAGE-MATCH CHECK
Compare:
Traffic / campaign message
Landing-page message
Any mismatch
Recommended correction

3. HERO SECTION
Provide:
Eyebrow if useful
Headline
Subheadline
Primary CTA
Secondary CTA if needed
Supporting microcopy

4. PROBLEM SECTION
Provide:
Section headline
Problem explanation
Relevant consequence
Transition to solution

5. SOLUTION SECTION
Explain:
What the offer is
How it addresses the problem
Why the approach matters

6. BENEFIT SECTION
For each benefit provide:
Benefit headline
Explanation
Supporting feature
Desired outcome

7. HOW IT WORKS
Create a simple step-by-step explanation.

8. USE CASES
Provide relevant customer situations.

9. DIFFERENTIATION SECTION
Explain credible reasons to consider the offer.

10. PROOF & TRUST SECTION
Recommend:
Available proof
Where to place it
Missing proof
Alternative trust elements

11. OBJECTION HANDLING
For each important objection provide:
Objection
Response
Evidence required

12. FAQ
Create decision-relevant questions and answers.
Mark unknown answers as "Information required."

13. CTA PLAN
Provide:
Primary CTA
Secondary CTA
Recommended placements
Final CTA copy

14. PAGE STRUCTURE
Provide the recommended section order and explain why.

15. VISUAL DIRECTION
Recommend useful visuals for each major section.

16. MICROCOPY
Provide useful:
Button text
Form guidance
Supporting reassurance where factual

17. CLAIM CHECK
For important claims provide:
Claim
Support status
Risk
Recommended wording

18. CONVERSION FRICTION CHECK
Identify possible points of confusion or hesitation.

19. A/B TEST IDEAS
Provide:
Element
Control
Variant
Hypothesis
Metric

20. INFORMATION REQUIRED
List missing facts needed before publication.

21. FINAL LANDING PAGE COPY
Assemble the recommended copy in page order so it can be used as a practical first draft.

IMPORTANT:
- Prioritize clarity over cleverness.
- Maintain message consistency with the traffic source.
- Do not invent features, results, offers or proof.
- Do not fabricate testimonials or customer numbers.
- Do not answer unknown FAQs with guesses.
- Use one clear primary conversion goal.
- Keep claims proportional to evidence.
- Build the page around the customer's decision process rather than a generic template.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Match the visitor's expectation",
    description:
      "Start with the ad, search or campaign message that brought the visitor to the page and make sure the hero immediately continues that conversation.",
  },
  {
    title: "Build one conversion path",
    description:
      "Choose a clear primary action and organize the page around the information, benefits, proof and objections needed to support that decision.",
  },
  {
    title: "Show value before adding persuasion",
    description:
      "Explain what the offer actually does and connect real features to customer outcomes before relying on slogans, social proof or promotional language.",
  },
  {
    title: "Remove conversion friction",
    description:
      "Check for unclear CTAs, message mismatch, unanswered questions, weak trust, unnecessary form fields and unsupported claims before publishing.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Page: Reorder Point Calculator.",
    "Audience: Small-business owners and inventory professionals.",
    "Traffic source: LinkedIn educational post.",
    "Customer problem: Uncertainty about when stock should be reordered.",
    "Capability: Calculates reorder point using demand, lead time and safety stock.",
    "Offer: Free online calculator.",
    "Primary CTA: Use the calculator.",
  ],

  output: [
    "HERO HEADLINE: Know When It's Time to Reorder Stock.",
    "SUBHEAD: Enter your demand, lead time and safety stock to calculate a practical reorder point for an inventory item.",
    "CTA: Calculate Your Reorder Point.",
    "PROBLEM SECTION: Replenishment decisions become harder when reorder timing is based only on instinct or inconsistent rules.",
    "HOW IT WORKS: Enter the required inventory values, calculate the reorder point, then use the result as one input in your replenishment planning.",
    "TRUST APPROACH: Show the formula, explain each input and provide a worked example rather than inventing testimonials.",
    "CLAIM CHECK: Do not promise that the calculator prevents stockouts because supplier reliability, demand accuracy and other operating conditions also affect availability.",
    "FINAL CTA: Ready to check an item? Enter your inventory data and calculate the reorder point.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "The first screen should answer the basic questions",
    description:
      "Visitors should quickly understand what the offer is, why it is relevant and what they can do next without having to interpret a clever slogan.",
  },
  {
    title: "Proof does not have to mean testimonials",
    description:
      "When customer evidence is unavailable, transparent methodology, demonstrations, screenshots, examples and clear explanations can still reduce uncertainty without fabricating social proof.",
  },
  {
    title: "Every section should support the decision",
    description:
      "Remove sections that exist only because other landing pages have them. Page length and structure should reflect the customer's actual information needs.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Landing Page Messaging Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Build conversion-focused landing page messaging with stronger headlines, benefits, CTAs, proof, objection handling, FAQs and message-match checks."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="MARKETING"
    title="Landing Page Messaging"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Plan and write clearer landing-page messaging around customer intent, value, benefits, proof, objections and one focused conversion action."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Landing pages",
      "Conversion copy",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Product pages",
      "Lead generation",
      "SaaS",
      "Online tools",
      "Service pages",
      "Campaign landing pages",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Turn campaign interest into a clear conversion path."
    introDescription="Match the visitor's expectation, explain the offer quickly, build a credible value story and organize the page around the information needed to take the next step."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Build clearer messaging for a free inventory calculator."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="A landing page converts by reducing uncertainty, not by adding more hype."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need ad messaging first?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Ad Copy Writer Prompt to create the campaign message and then carry the same promise, audience and CTA into the landing page."
    warningTitle="Landing-page copy must reflect the real offer"
    warningText="Verify product capabilities, pricing, guarantees, testimonials, performance results, savings and comparative claims before publishing. Mark unknown information rather than filling gaps with persuasive-sounding assumptions."
  />
</BaseLayout>